9. MAPPING CANDIDATE ENGINE
==================================================

Before calling the LLM, create deterministic candidate matching.

For every target field, compare available source fields.

Use a combination of:

1. Exact field-name matching
2. Normalized-name matching
3. Description matching
4. Token similarity
5. Pattern similarity
6. Data-type compatibility
7. Sample-value compatibility
8. Previously approved mappings

Example:

Source:
VEND_ID
VEND_NM
PAY_CD
CNTRY_NM

Target:
LIFNR
NAME1
ZTERM
LAND1

The candidate engine should produce ranked candidates.

Example:

LAND1

Candidate      Score
CNTRY_NM       84
COUNTRY        79
REGION_CD      32

Keep scoring implementation independent from the LLM.


==================================================
10. AI MAPPING ENGINE
==================================================

After candidate shortlisting, call the configurable LLM service.

Provide the LLM with:

Target field
Target description
Target datatype
Maximum length
Mandatory indicator
Domain
Candidate source fields
Candidate descriptions
Source datatype
Source sample values
Previously approved mapping if available

Require STRUCTURED JSON output.

Example:

{
  "target_field": "LAND1",
  "source_field": "CNTRY_NM",
  "confidence_score": 88,
  "transformation_type": "VALUE_MAPPING",
  "value_mapping_required": true,
  "rationale": "Source contains country names while target expects a country code.",
  "alternatives": []
}

Validate the returned JSON before persisting it.

IMPORTANT:

AI recommendations are recommendations only.

Never automatically approve mappings merely because the confidence is high.

Provide human review.


